<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taspar Qaghan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 581</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Tatpar Qaghan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taspar Qaghan was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a> from 572 to 581, a son of the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a> and successor of his brother Muqan. He extracted large tributes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> from the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, whose rulers he reportedly called his two dutiful sons in the south. He was also a notable patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> among the Turks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taspar-qaghan.jpg" alt="Taspar Qaghan" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:He-ba-mue · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kloster_zezerleg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
